--- !u!114 &11400000
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: e9620f8c34305754d8cc9a7e49e852d9, type: 3}
  m_Name: Chapter 3 text_en
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Localization::UnityEngine.Localization.Tables.StringTable
  m_LocaleId:
    m_Code: en
  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: f76c707db27a34340adaa1b3eb8e72d8, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 5602299904
    m_Localized: Look at that, we've already reached the <color=orange>ALU</color>!
      That's surely a familiar component for you too. So, should I give you a little
      lecture, or do you already know what's up?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 192202690560
    m_Localized: Then off into battle! But don't be scared of the <color=#48DBFB>big
      Four</color> - it doesn't actually do anything, it just passes the value <color=#48DBFB>4</color>
      on to the <color=orange>Bus</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 238419726336
    m_Localized: The <color=orange>ALU</color> is the <color=orange>Arithmetic Logic
      Unit</color>. That means it can perform <color=#48DBFB>arithmetic operations</color>
      (addition and subtraction) as well as <color=#48DBFB>logic operations</color>
      (logical OR and logical AND).
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 283768541184
    m_Localized: Do you have any questions about that?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 327846481920
    m_Localized: <anim:wave>Great that you're asking!</anim> These are the basic
      logic operations. Every number in our computer is stored as a sequence of <color=#48DBFB>zeros</color>
      and <color=#48DBFB>ones</color>. It doesn't matter at all whether it's an integer
      or a floating-point number!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 372289327104
    m_Localized: "These two operations are called <color=orange>\u201Ebitwise\u201C</color>,
      because they work directly with the zeros and ones - i.e. the <color=#48DBFB>bits</color>.
      And they always compare just one bit at a time. That means they go through
      the number \u201Efrom right to left\u201C one after another. And they're called
      <color=orange>\u201Elogical\u201C</color> because they're used to build formulas
      and so on."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 415058644992
    m_Localized: 'To understand which number comes out in the end, for example with
      <color=#48DBFB>3 & 16 = ???</color>, you need to write out both numbers as
      <color=#48DBFB>zeros</color> and <color=#48DBFB>ones</color>. Then write them
      right-aligned one below the other and apply the following:'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 480452038656
    m_Localized: In order for it to do that, it's sent one of four <color=yellow>control
      signals</color>:\n<color=#48DBFB>00</color> - Addition\n<color=#48DBFB>01</color>
      - Subtraction\n<color=#48DBFB>10</color> - logical bitwise AND\n<color=#48DBFB>11</color>
      - logical bitwise OR
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 534692777984
    m_Localized: 'When we use <color=yellow>& (AND)</color>  : A <color=#48DBFB>one</color>
      only comes out if there''s a 1 on top <anim:shake><color=yellow>AND</color></anim>
      a 1 on the bottom.\nWhen we use <color=yellow>| (OR)</color> : A <color=#48DBFB>one</color>
      always comes out, <anim:shake><color=yellow>except</color></anim> when there
      are two <color=red>zeros</color> there.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 648488439808
    m_Localized: I still know exactly how it works.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 834849755136
    m_Localized: Why not? Let's go over it.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 918081523712
    m_Localized: Nope, all clear!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 973299535872
    m_Localized: And what exactly is this logical (and also bitwise) AND and OR?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1054937468928
    m_Localized: But how do you understand what comes out as the result?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1117763948544
    m_Localized: Okey-doke
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1321091223552
    m_Localized: Well, that was actually pretty easy, wasn't it?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1399747006464
    m_Localized: Well, let's see how serious you are about that. You did claim that
      you know everything about <color=orange>logic AND</color> and <color=orange>logic
      OR</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1440834408448
    m_Localized: Alright, let's <anim:wave><color=yellow>shed some light on this</color></anim>!
      Let's take the number <color=#48DBFB>5</color> as an example.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1496983556096
    m_Localized: 'To apply <color=orange>AND</color>, we simply write one number
      above the other and take a close look:

      If <anim:shake><color=yellow>both
      bits</color></anim> in a column are a <color=yellow>one</color>, the result
      is also <color=yellow>one</color>.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1535130750976
    m_Localized: 'Here''s an example:

      11001

      10101

      -----

      <color=green>1</color>000<color=green>1</color>'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1868762468352
    m_Localized: "With <color=orange>OR</color> it's exactly the opposite:\r\nIf
      <anim:shake><color=yellow>at least one</color></anim> number in the column
      is a <color=yellow>one</color> \u2014 then the result is also a <color=yellow>one</color>!\r\n11001\r\n10101\r\n-----\r\n<color=green>111</color>0<color=green>1</color>"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2017790283776
    m_Localized: Time for a little <anim:wave><color=yellow>warm-up</color></anim>!
      I've prepared a simple case for you. Let's see if you know what to do.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2093845598208
    m_Localized: <anim:wave><color=yellow>Ok, good luck!</color></anim>
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2423652110336
    m_Localized: Come on, don't keep me in suspense!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2542820675584
    m_Localized: Hmm, I don't remember saying that...
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2676115656704
    m_Localized: '<anim:wave>So, how did it go?</anim>

      Did you notice that
      the last task could be solved with both <color=orange>AND</color> and <color=orange>OR</color>?'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2795569434624
    m_Localized: Well, yeah. The chance of getting it right through pure guessing
      was a <color=yellow>whopping 50 %</color>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2844693123072
    m_Localized: Sooo, now let's set the abstract numbers aside and look at something
      closer to reality.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2896467611648
    m_Localized: This time you'll get a rough idea of how the update process of the
      <color=orange>PC register</color> works.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2939786383360
    m_Localized: '<anim:wave><color=yellow>Glad you''re asking! :D</color></anim>

      <color=orange>PC</color>
      stands for <color=orange>Program Counter</color> - that''s the <anim:wave>register</anim>
      that stores the <anim:wave>address</anim> of the current line in the program.
      So at the start it points to where the program begins, and after that it keeps
      incrementing to fetch new lines of <color=orange>program code</color> from
      memory.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3080257818624
    m_Localized: 'And of course we all remember exactly: If the current <color=orange>PC
      address</color> is 16, then the next address is... <anim:wave>weeeeell... ????</anim>'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3237993009152
    m_Localized: <anim:shake><color=yellow>Ah-ah! The correct answer is 20.</color></anim>
      That's because every line, every <color=orange>assembly instruction</color>,
      is stored in one <color=yellow>WORD</color>. That means we need <color=#48DBFB>4
      bytes</color> to store a single instruction of our code.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3357484535808
    m_Localized: 'That in turn means: to move on from the current instruction, which
      starts at address <color=#48DBFB>16</color>, we have to skip the <color=#48DBFB>4
      bytes</color> that contain the current instruction.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3413486882816
    m_Localized: Alright, you're ready for the <anim:wave>next level</anim>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3550758064128
    m_Localized: So what is a PC?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3589714759680
    m_Localized: Cool... ?
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1171453830254592
    m_Localized: If you can't find anything in common in the <color=#48DBFB>binary
      notation</color>, there's always the option of simply trying out all the <color=yellow>ALU
      operations</color> one after another and checking the result <anim:wave><color=yellow>:D</color></anim> 
      You can reset the <color=orange>clock cycles</color> at any time, after all!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1170347028926464
    m_Localized: 'Think about it: what do these two <color=yellow>numbers</color>
      have in common?



      If nothing comes to mind, try writing them out in
      <color=#48DBFB>binary notation</color>!'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1171761117548544
    m_Localized: 'If you can''t find anything in common in the <color=#48DBFB>binary
      notation</color>, there''s always the option of simply trying out all the <color=yellow>ALU
      operations</color> one after another and checking the result <anim:wave><color=yellow>:D</color></anim>



      You
      can reset the <color=orange>clock cycles</color> at any time, after all!'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1171790016303104
    m_Localized: 'There are many ways to solve this level, but here''s the <color=yellow>simplest
      tip</color>: first you need to get a <color=#48DBFB>0</color> in the <color=yellow>register</color>,
      and then add <color=#48DBFB>4</color> <color=#48DBFB>twice</color>!'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2208935208308736
    m_Localized: 'Goal: \r\nWrite value 8 to Register 1'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2209008386330624
    m_Localized: 'Goal: \r\nWrite value 1 to Register 3'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2209018133893120
    m_Localized: 'Goal: \r\nWrite value 7 to Register 3'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
